Z||000008||dman||05-10-2000||04:03 PM||||Hello bHooper. I don't think you want to use ATF as hydraulic (brake/clutch) fluid replacement. I'm surprised the thread you read would indicate something like that.<P>I strongly advise you to stick with DOT3 or DOT4 brake fluid. I'm pretty sure a forum member here has been spending a good amount of time repairing their system due to using the ATF.<P>ATF will destroy your seals from what I understand.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||05-10-2000||04:15 PM||||DO NOT USE ATF||||reg||1
Z||000010||bHooper||05-10-2000||04:22 PM||||Ok, so maybe I want use ATF.  However, alot of that thread was spent praising the results of ATF... ozzy, how's your car workig with the stuff?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Mark||05-10-2000||06:34 PM||||That "pipe to nowhere" running from the air cleaner housing is the result of a recall modification to the crankcase ventilation system.  This "open ended" pipe should be plugged to prevent unfiltered air from being drawn into the engine (remember, air flows from the INSIDE to the OUTSIDE of the air filter element, therefore the "open ended" pipe is downstream of the filter element!!!)<P>Also, DO NOT USE ATF.  It is incompatible with the rubber seals in the clutch master/slave.||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||05-10-2000||09:03 PM||||Super!  I will plug the hole, 1st thing!<P>Ok, ok, no ATF!<P>Also, as of 45 min ago, I hav <B>ALL</B> cradle bolts fully installed and the cradle/back end of the car is situated nicely on 2 pair of jackstands  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000013||Phil||05-10-2000||10:23 PM||||In later threads it was found that atf eventually destroyed the rubber seals in both the master and slave. I believe everybody has switched back.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Fret Naught||05-11-2000||01:39 AM||||Very COOL!!! I have one of those mysterious pipes to nowhere, and looked all over for where it went, I guess that recall has been performed. thanks||||reg||14
