Z||000016||bHooper||05-12-2000||07:45 PM||||ok, so now i have my bud's GT parked in my driveway (broke), giving me a "working" manual!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I have discovered whymy clutch pedal has so much play... the slave cylinder is fully extended!<P><BR>Going to Advanced to buy a new one.<P>Problem 3, taken care of.<P>Cant wate for my other parts, its driveing me crazy.<P>soon... soon... the car will be on the road, soon<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000017||Richard Parnell||05-12-2000||08:40 PM||||If you buy the master and slave for the clutch system be absolutely sure you get the correct ones.  I bought some from one of the on-line parts places (can't remember which) at a pretty high price and the slave was the wrong type.  Called George at the Fiero shop and he knew which one I needed by the year, modle, engine tranny combination of the car at an amazing price.  And yes DON'T use ATF.  It melted the seals and even the rubber thingy inside the master reservoir cap. It looked chewing gum that you've just stepped on a hot day. What a mess.||||reg||7
Z||000018||mrfiero||05-12-2000||09:30 PM||||I can tell you that the slave you get from Advance Auto will likely be a cast unit.  These are not worth the money or headache, so I would highly recommend against buying it.  Go to you GM dealer and order a new factory replacement slave.  It will cost a lot more than the aftermarket one, but it will pay for itself in the long run (bleeds correctly the first time and won't break!).<P>Just my 2 cents.<P>BTW....the same goes for the master cylinder......get original GM stuff on your clutch hydraulics and you'll be a happier person.  Guaranteed.<BR> ||||reg||4
Z||000019||bHooper||05-13-2000||04:57 PM||||Here's what happend with the slave cylinder, today.  I bought a rebuild kit from NAPA, came home and tried to free the SC from the mount... no luck.  So I am going to dremel the frozen nut off and replace the clutch line (I guess), it is also "frozen and the nut is buggered.<P>Pontiac says that the SC is $150 or so, but they will let me have it at wholesale for $115!  I will order one on monday!||||reg||1
Z||000020||Raydar||05-13-2000||06:27 PM||||Hoop,<BR>Congrats on getting your car back together! Haven't said anything because I didn't really have any info that might help. Sounds like you've got it well on the way. Way ta go!||||reg||1
Z||000021||batboy||05-13-2000||07:46 PM||||Here's what the Fiero Secrets section of The Big Fiero Book says about clutch slave/master cylinders.<P>"If you want to bolt in a brand new part, don't go to your GM dealer. They can get it for you, but the price will shock you...Go to your local parts store and ask for the BENDIX brand (only) replacement part(s). These parts are new and are the same, or better quality than what you would get from your GM dealer."<P>I would also add that the braided flexible slave line will be expensive. If you can save it, it might be worth it. Use a pair of good vice-grips and penetrating oil. If the shoulders are already rounded, what do you have to lose. Also, take the slave unit off and take it with you to get your part so you can compare the two, it might save you another trip.||||reg||1
Z||000022||bHooper||05-13-2000||10:08 PM||||thanks, everyone!<P>Batboy, I did go to Advance Auto, today.  They pulled one out, then said it was for the 4spd 2.8L car, not the 5 spd.  It looked mostly like mine, except that it was cast iron, and the "line in" was at a different angle (slightly).  So I didnt buy it.<P>Also, do replacement SC's come with the rod inside the boot?  This one did not.<P>also when I bought the Rebuild kit, the NAPA  guy started asking if I had 15/16" or 13/16" slave something or nother.<P>What is the correct part/s for the 5 spd GT's?<P>Thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000023||Fiasco||05-15-2000||02:06 AM||||That's the bore of the cylinder. I'm not sure which is for which but just make sure it's the same bore diameter as the one you're replacing.<BR>I put a cast iron slave in mine some time ago and it's doing fine, but maybe the guys are right and it won't last as long, I don't know. <BR>And I also had to vice-grip the line off... oh well- so I got a round nut on my car. <P>Seems like any thread having to do with clutch hydraulics gets pretty long!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Dan||05-15-2000||09:42 AM|||| Frozen hydraulic line? Try heat as in propane torch. Works great on brake lines,bleeders ect.When assembling, use anti-sieze to prevent reoccurance. Just my nickles worth.||||reg||1
